# Summary
Introduced: the commit makes previously local Unix-socket control-plane RPCs reachable over TCP while not enforcing a mandatory authentication layer for all TCP RPCs. Existing optional auth semantics for runtime-map reads become a network information disclosure because the new TLS listener uses no client auth and accepts requests before application auth rejects only credential-required operation classes.
The commit adds TLS/TCP control-plane listeners, but the TLS server is configured with no client certificate authentication and relies on the existing application-level control-plane auth verifier. That verifier only requires runtime-map/read-only RPC authentication when frontend credentials are configured. In supported replicated static configurations that have control-plane/admin and storage-node credentials but no frontend credentials, unauthenticated network clients can complete TLS with the required ALPN and send unsigned read-only control-plane frames. Those requests are accepted with response_auth = None and can return RuntimeMapSnapshot, RuntimeMapStatus/Diagnostics, PgRuntimeMapSnapshot, and PendingMetadataCommandRecoveries. This exposes internal cluster topology and recovery metadata over the newly network-reachable TCP endpoint. Mutating admin operations and storage heartbeats still require their configured credentials, so the impact is primarily confidentiality of control-plane metadata rather than object data or integrity.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`ffa06960`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/ffa06960) (`Require application auth on control-plane TCP`).

TCP control-plane listeners now derive mandatory application authentication from the bound transport in `BoundControlPlaneRpcListener::apply_limits()`. This requirement is independent of which credential roles happen to be configured, so an unsigned TCP request is rejected before Raft admission or authority dispatch and no response is published. Unix listeners retain the existing local compatibility behavior instead of inheriting the network-listener requirement.

The verifier also accepts an admin-scoped credential for read-only runtime-map operations when mandatory TCP authentication is active. This keeps admin-only control-plane deployments operable without weakening role authorization: the authenticated principal must still be authorized for the concrete operation, and response signing uses the verified request context. Static manifest validation rejects ordinary TCP control-plane listeners with neither a frontend nor admin credential and rejects TCP clock-recovery listeners without an admin credential.

The regression `tcp_control_plane_listener_requires_auth_when_role_is_unconfigured` exercises a real bound TLS/TCP listener with the production ALPN and an admin-only verifier. It sends an unsigned runtime-map request and proves that the request receives no response and reaches neither Raft admission nor authority dispatch. Focused verifier coverage proves that mandatory authentication rejects an unsigned read and accepts an admin-signed read. Static configuration regressions cover both ordinary and recovery listener credential requirements. Workspace formatting, strict clippy, the storage-cluster boundary check, and the full 7,488-test nextest suite passed for the fixing commit.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the new TCP control-plane transport permits TLS handshakes without client certificates and relies only on ALPN before reading RPC frames.
- [x] Confirm a supported auth verifier with storage-node and admin credentials but no frontend credentials reports `requires_frontend_runtime_map_auth() == false` while other credential classes are configured.
- [x] Confirm unsigned read-only runtime-map RPCs are accepted with `response_auth = None` in that configuration.
- [x] Confirm an unauthenticated TLS/TCP client can retrieve a `RuntimeMapSnapshot` containing internal node endpoint metadata.
- [x] Confirm the opposite case still rejects unsigned runtime-map reads when frontend credentials are configured, limiting the bug to the no-frontend-credential configuration.
## Report
Validated the finding. I added targeted temporary PoC tests and built with debug symbols. The strongest reproduction is a TLS/TCP PoC in `crates/argmin-s3/src/static_cluster_config.rs:7345`: it configures the server with `.with_no_client_auth()`, an ALPN-capable client with no client certificate, an auth verifier containing storage-node and admin credentials but no frontend credentials, then performs an unsigned `RuntimeMapSnapshot` over the TCP frame transport. Command output: `POC unsigned TLS/TCP RuntimeMapSnapshot leaked 10.0.0.8:19001/tcp-control; frontend_auth_required=false` and the test passed. A storage-layer PoC in `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:25512` independently shows the same verifier accepts an unsigned `RuntimeMapSnapshot` and returns a plain decodable response containing `10.0.0.7:19001/internal-control`. The original code supports the causal chain: TCP server config disables TLS client auth at `crates/argmin-s3/src/static_cluster_config.rs:1847-1856`; the TCP worker only completes TLS and checks ALPN before reading control-plane frames at `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:5817-5839`; runtime-map auth is required only when frontend credentials exist at `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:13105-13116`; read-only RPCs fall through to `(payload, None)` when frontend auth is not required at `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:14218-14235`; and `RuntimeMapSnapshot` builds a response using that `response_auth`, which is unsigned when `None`, at `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:14319-14336` and `14571-14588`. I also ran the existing contrast test `authenticated_control_plane_rejects_missing_frontend_runtime_map_auth`, which passed, confirming unsigned reads are rejected when frontend credentials are configured. Valgrind and gdb were unavailable (`command not found`). I used lldb non-interactively; the captured backtrace stops in `verify_control_plane_unix_request` with `kind = RuntimeMapSnapshot`, called from `build_control_plane_unix_response_with_auth`, then the PoC completes successfully.

